From: SAGA <saga@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 9 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] block/adios: top-app IO deadline bonus, self-activated via in-kernel timer

REQUIRES the "adios" addon to already be at v3.3.4-SAGA
(adios-android14-6.1-v3.3.4.patch, the merged base that folds in the
former adios-tunable LM/sysfs extension). Context matched byte-for-
byte against that exact file -- verified with `patch --fuzz=0`, zero
rejects, zero offset.

THIS PATCH REPLACES the previous two-file version of this feature
(0003-adios-topapp-deadline-bonus.patch +
0004-adios-topapp-self-activation-timer.patch). Delete both of those
if present. Reason for the replacement, and for merging into a single
file: those two were built and verified against the OLD
adios-android14-6.1-v3.2.0.patch base. When the "adios" addon's base
patch was independently upgraded to v3.3.4-SAGA (merging in the former
adios-tunable extension), block/adios.c grew from ~2100 to ~3100 lines
and several functions were restructured. `patch --fuzz=3` still
"succeeded" applying the old files against the new base -- with
offsets up to 1000 lines -- but landed content in a subtly wrong
location inside adios_init_sched(), causing an immediate NULL/invalid
pointer dereference (EL1 translation fault) at device probe on every
boot (confirmed via pstore console-ramoops: crash at
adios_init_sched+0x48, called from sd_probe -> device_add_disk ->
elevator_init_mq -> blk_mq_init_sched, ~5s into boot). Splitting this
into two separately-versioned files was what let that drift happen
undetected; one file, explicitly version-pinned to v3.3.4-SAGA, is
harder to silently apply against the wrong base.

--- What this actually does (unchanged in intent from before) ---

Part A -- classification + deadline discount (add_to_dl_tree):
adios_req_is_topapp() reads req_get_ioprio(rq) (rq->ioprio, set from
the submitting bio at allocation time) and classifies a request as
latency-critical if its class is IOPRIO_CLASS_RT, or IOPRIO_CLASS_BE
at level 0-1. This is the same signal BFQ/mq-deadline already use for
this purpose. struct request has no cgroup reference by the time it
reaches the elevator (blkcg is a separate controller attachment from
the task's cpu/uclamp cgroup, and the submitting task_struct isn't
reliably available this late), so this does NOT use
task_group()->latency_sensitive the way the BORE-side patches do --
that signal isn't available at this layer.
Whether this has any effect at all depends on whether your Android
build actually sets IO priority for the top-app cgroup (some
task_profiles.json configs do via SetIoPriority/ioprio_set(); many
don't). If it doesn't, adios_req_is_topapp() never returns true and
this is a safe no-op.
add_to_dl_tree() subtracts a bounded, sysfs-tunable nanosecond bonus
(topapp_deadline_bonus) from the computed deadline right before
quantization, for both sync and async requests -- moving matching
requests earlier in the deadline-ordered RB-tree. Bounded by min_t so
it can never push a deadline negative/wrap.

Part B -- self-activation timer (the actual bootloop fix):
topapp_deadline_bonus starts at 0 (disabled) -- there's no meaningful
"top-app" during early boot, so activating immediately only risks
catching boot-time daemons that happen to share an elevated ioprio for
unrelated reasons (which is what caused the original bootloop; a
different, separate crash from the version-mismatch one above -- see
git history / earlier review rounds on this addon for that one).
A one-shot timer_list (topapp_activate_timer), armed inside
adios_init_sched() the same way the existing update_timer already is,
fires topapp_activation_delay_ms (default 90000, i.e. 90s) after this
scheduler attaches to its queue and flips topapp_deadline_bonus over
to topapp_deadline_bonus_target (default 4ms) by itself -- no init.rc,
no device-tree change, no separate kernel module. del_timer_sync() in
adios_exit_sched() prevents any use-after-free if the queue is torn
down before the delay elapses.
Note on the timer's epoch: on builds using
CONFIG_MQ_IOSCHED_ADIOS_DEFAULT, adios_init_sched() runs at storage-
queue probe time -- itself very early in kernel boot, well before
Android userspace/init starts -- so in practice 90s-from-that-point
lands close to 90s-from-kernel-boot on such builds. It does not track
Android's sys.boot_completed and isn't meant to.
All three new values (topapp_deadline_bonus,
topapp_deadline_bonus_target, topapp_activation_delay_ms) are exposed
read/write via sysfs, same SYSFS_ULL_DECL/AD_ATTR_RW pattern as every
other ADIOS tunable -- re-tunable, or force-activatable early by
writing topapp_deadline_bonus directly, without a rebuild.

Verified: `patch -p1 --fuzz=0` against a freshly reconstructed
block/adios.c (adios-android14-6.1-v3.3.4.patch applied first) --
zero rejects, zero fuzz, zero offset.

Signed-off-by: SAGA <saga@users.noreply.github.com>
---
diff --git a/block/adios.c b/block/adios.c
+++ b/block/adios.c
@@ -20,6 +20,7 @@
 #include <linux/string.h>
 #include <linux/list_sort.h>
 #include <linux/rcupdate.h>
+#include <linux/ioprio.h>
 #include <linux/sched.h>
 #include <linux/workqueue.h>
 
@@ -200,6 +201,17 @@
 };
 
 // Latency targets for each operation type
+/*
+ * Starts at 0 (disabled). There's no meaningful "top-app" this early,
+ * so activating immediately only ever risks catching boot-time
+ * daemons that happen to share an elevated ioprio. Self-activates via
+ * topapp_activate_timer below, topapp_activation_delay_ms after this
+ * scheduler attaches to its queue -- fully in-kernel, no userspace
+ * signal, no init.rc, no module needed.
+ */
+static u64 default_topapp_deadline_bonus;
+static u64 default_topapp_deadline_bonus_target = 4 * NSEC_PER_MSEC;
+static u64 default_topapp_activation_delay_ms = 90000;
 static u64 default_latency_target[ADIOS_OPTYPES] = {
 	[ADIOS_READ]    =     2ULL * NSEC_PER_MSEC,
 	[ADIOS_WRITE]   =  2000ULL * NSEC_PER_MSEC,
@@ -393,6 +405,10 @@
 	u64 global_latency_window;
 	u64 compliance_flags;
 	u64 latency_target[ADIOS_OPTYPES];
+	u64 topapp_deadline_bonus;
+	u64 topapp_deadline_bonus_target;
+	u64 topapp_activation_delay_ms;
+	struct timer_list topapp_activate_timer;
 	u32 batch_limit[ADIOS_OPTYPES];
 	u32 batch_actual_max_size[ADIOS_OPTYPES];
 	u32 batch_actual_max_total;
@@ -1248,6 +1264,25 @@
 	return true;
 }
 
+/*
+ * True when the request's IO priority marks it as belonging to a
+ * latency-critical submitter -- RT class outright, or the top tiers
+ * of the BE class (Android's task_profiles.json commonly assigns one
+ * of these to the top-app cgroup via ioprio_set()/SetIoPriority when
+ * configured to do so; harmless no-op otherwise, since default class
+ * for unconfigured processes is IOPRIO_CLASS_NONE/BE-4).
+ */
+static inline bool adios_req_is_topapp(struct request *rq) {
+	unsigned short ioprio = req_get_ioprio(rq);
+	unsigned short class = IOPRIO_PRIO_CLASS(ioprio);
+
+	if (class == IOPRIO_CLASS_RT)
+		return true;
+	if (class == IOPRIO_CLASS_BE && IOPRIO_PRIO_DATA(ioprio) <= 1)
+		return true;
+	return false;
+}
+
 // Add a request to the deadline-sorted red-black tree
 static void add_to_dl_tree(
 		struct adios_data *ad, bool dl_idx, struct request *rq) {
@@ -1301,6 +1336,9 @@
 			rd->deadline += rd->pred_lat;
 	}
 
+	if (ad->topapp_deadline_bonus && adios_req_is_topapp(rq))
+		rd->deadline -= min_t(u64, rd->deadline, ad->topapp_deadline_bonus);
+
 	// Now quantize the deadline (-> dlg->deadline == RB-Tree key)
 	deadline = rd->deadline & ~((1ULL << ADIOS_QUANTUM_SHIFT) - 1);
 
@@ -2006,6 +2044,19 @@
 		schedule_work(&ad->shrink_work);
 }
 
+/*
+ * One-shot: fires topapp_activation_delay_ms after this scheduler was
+ * attached to its queue. Just flips the live bonus over to its
+ * target value; does not re-arm.
+ */
+static void topapp_activate_timer_callback(struct timer_list *t) {
+	struct adios_data *ad = from_timer(ad, t, topapp_activate_timer);
+
+	WRITE_ONCE(ad->topapp_deadline_bonus, ad->topapp_deadline_bonus_target);
+	pr_info("adios: topapp_deadline_bonus activated (%llu ns) after %llums\n",
+		ad->topapp_deadline_bonus_target, ad->topapp_activation_delay_ms);
+}
+
 // CHATGPT-REVIEW FIX #4: force_shrink_model() moved here from the
 // timer callback. Runs in a workqueue's own process context, not
 // softirq and not the I/O completion hot path -- the allocation
@@ -2336,6 +2387,10 @@
 
 	eq->elevator_data = ad;
 
+	ad->topapp_deadline_bonus = default_topapp_deadline_bonus;
+	ad->topapp_deadline_bonus_target = default_topapp_deadline_bonus_target;
+	ad->topapp_activation_delay_ms = default_topapp_activation_delay_ms;
+
 	ad->is_rotational = !blk_queue_nonrot(q);
 	ad->global_latency_window = (ad->is_rotational)?
 		default_global_latency_window_rotational:
@@ -2409,6 +2464,12 @@
 	INIT_LIST_HEAD(&ad->barrier_queue);
 
 	timer_setup(&ad->update_timer, update_timer_callback, 0);
+
+	timer_setup(&ad->topapp_activate_timer, topapp_activate_timer_callback, 0);
+	if (ad->topapp_activation_delay_ms)
+		mod_timer(&ad->topapp_activate_timer,
+			jiffies + msecs_to_jiffies((unsigned int)ad->topapp_activation_delay_ms));
+
 	INIT_WORK(&ad->shrink_work, shrink_work_fn);
 
 	/* We dispatch from request queue wide instead of hw queue */
@@ -2449,6 +2510,7 @@
 	struct adios_data *ad = e->elevator_data;
 
 	del_timer_sync(&ad->update_timer);
+	del_timer_sync(&ad->topapp_activate_timer);
 	cancel_work_sync(&ad->shrink_work);
 
 	WARN_ON_ONCE(!list_empty(&ad->barrier_queue));
@@ -2641,6 +2703,9 @@
 
 SYSFS_ULL_DECL(global_latency_window, 0, ULLONG_MAX)
 SYSFS_ULL_DECL(compliance_flags, 0, ULLONG_MAX)
+SYSFS_ULL_DECL(topapp_deadline_bonus, 0, NSEC_PER_SEC)
+SYSFS_ULL_DECL(topapp_deadline_bonus_target, 0, NSEC_PER_SEC)
+SYSFS_ULL_DECL(topapp_activation_delay_ms, 0, 600000ULL)
 
 #define SYSFS_INT_DECL(field, min_val, max_val) \
 static ssize_t adios_##field##_show( \
@@ -3023,6 +3088,9 @@
 	AD_ATTR_RW(lm_samples_threshold),
 
 	AD_ATTR_RW(read_priority),
+	AD_ATTR_RW(topapp_deadline_bonus),
+	AD_ATTR_RW(topapp_deadline_bonus_target),
+	AD_ATTR_RW(topapp_activation_delay_ms),
 
 	AD_ATTR_WO(reset_bq_stats),
 	AD_ATTR_WO(reset_lat_model),
